LangGraph Concepts Explained in Very Simple Terms

Imagine you're running a bus ticket booking office.

A customer comes in and says:

"I want to travel from Chennai to Madurai tomorrow."

Several employees work together to complete the booking.

This is exactly how LangGraph works.

1. What is a Node?
Simple Definition

A Node is one step (or one worker) in the workflow.

Think of a node as one employee doing one specific job.

| Employee   | Job                          |
| ---------- | ---------------------------- |
| Employee 1 | Ask where you want to travel |
| Employee 2 | Search available buses       |
| Employee 3 | Check seat availability      |
| Employee 4 | Collect payment              |
| Employee 5 | Print the ticket             |

Each employee = One Node

In LangGraph
Node 1
Understand User

↓

Node 2
Search Bus

↓

Node 3
Check Seats

↓

Node 4
Payment

↓

Node 5
Generate Ticket

Each box is a Node.

Another Example

Restaurant chatbot:

Customer Orders Pizza
       │
       ▼
Understand Order

↓

Check Menu

↓

Calculate Price

↓

Take Payment

↓

Confirm Order

Every step is a Node.

====

2. What is State?

This is the most important concept in LangGraph.

Simple Definition

State is the shared notebook that stores all information collected during the workflow.

Imagine every employee in the bus office shares the same notebook.

The first employee writes:

Name: Ravi

The next employee adds:

From: Chennai

The next adds:

To: Madurai

The next adds:

Date: Tomorrow

The next adds:

Seat: A12

The notebook keeps growing.

That notebook is the State.

Example

Initially:

State

{}

After the first node:

State

Name = Ravi

After the second node:

State

Name = Ravi
From = Chennai

After the third node:

State

Name = Ravi
From = Chennai
To = Madurai

After payment:

State

Name = Ravi
From = Chennai
To = Madurai
Seat = A12
Price = ₹850
Payment = Success

Every node can read and update the same state.

Without State

Employee 2 asks again:

"What's your name?"

Employee 3 asks again:

"Where are you travelling?"

Very annoying.

With State

Employee 2 simply reads the notebook.

No need to ask again.

====

3. What is Orchestration?

This sounds complicated, but the idea is simple.

Simple Definition

Orchestration means managing and coordinating all the nodes so they work together in the correct order.

Imagine an orchestra.

There are:

Piano
Guitar
Violin
Drums
Flute

Someone tells each musician:

when to start,
when to stop,
who plays next.

That person is the orchestra conductor.

LangGraph

LangGraph is the conductor.

It tells each node:

Now Search Bus

↓

Now Check Seats

↓

Now Payment

↓

Now Generate Ticket

LangGraph decides:

which node runs,
when it runs,
what information it receives,
what happens next.

This coordination is called orchestration.

Real-Life Example

Imagine building a house.

Different workers are involved.

| Worker      | Job         |
| ----------- | ----------- |
| Engineer    | Design      |
| Mason       | Build walls |
| Electrician | Wiring      |
| Plumber     | Pipes       |
| Painter     | Painting    |


f everyone starts at the same time, it becomes chaotic.

Someone must coordinate them.

That coordination is orchestration.

=======

Bus Booking Example

Customer:

I need one ticket.

LangGraph (Orchestrator):

Step 1
Understand Request

↓

Step 2
Search Bus

↓

Step 3
Seats Available?

↓

If YES

Go to Payment

↓

If NO

Search Another Bus

↓

Payment

↓

Ticket

↓

SMS

LangGraph manages the flow from one step to the next.

